/* POV passage, her side of the task Extra Friendly (task_sister_be_nice_to_brian), stage 0: the first time she keeps the
   hardware guy company because I asked her to. Opened with the CCTV arrow while the task runs, or from her end-of-day
   report. Effects are in the task's onComplete.
   Proposal 6.5b: a comedy with a small sting. Her first order about another man. Coffee, ten minutes of capacitors, and
   the soldering iron collection her report line is about. Under the jokes: he asked her to be nice to another guy, and
   she does it because he asked.
   Clips (images/characters/sister/tasks/, activity extra_friendly0): she brings the coffee; she listens with her chin in
   her hand; the soldering irons */
<<silent>><<SetBackground $locations[13].img>><</silent>>
<<PovHeader $sister>>
Okay. Be nice to $brian.name. I can be nice to $brian.name. I'm nice to everybody, it's basically my whole personality, ask anyone, ask the guy at the bagel place who gets a smiley face on his tip receipt every single morning.

It's just that nobody has ever had to ask me before.

$player.name said it like it was nothing. Like asking me to water a plant. Be extra friendly to $brian.name for a while, he needs a little attention, and then he went back to his screen. And I said sure, of course, because what am I going to say? No, I don't want to, because I'd rather you were the one who wanted my attention?

So. Coffee. I asked $mom.callName how $brian.name takes it, because apparently she knows that, she knows everybody's coffee, it's a little terrifying. Two sugars. I carry it over with both hands like it's an offering, and I pull the spare chair up to his desk, and I sit.

<<silently>>
<<set _msgs = [
{ who: $sister, text: "Hi! I brought you a coffee. Two sugars. I have sources." },
{ who: $brian, text: "Oh. Uh. Thank you. You didn't, uh. Thank you." },
{ text: "He takes it without looking at me and puts it down on the far side of his keyboard, very carefully, like it might go off." },
{ who: $sister, text: "So what are you making? It looks important. It has a lot of little... things." },
{ who: $brian, text: "Capacitors." },
{ who: $sister, text: "Capacitors. Sure. Love those. What do they do?" },
]>>
<</silently>>
<<Chat _msgs>>

That was a mistake. A sweet mistake, but a mistake.

For ten minutes $brian.name tells me about capacitors. He tells the circuit board, mostly, but I'm the one listening. They store charge and let it go when you need it, and some of them are ceramic, and some of them can kill you if you touch them wrong, even when everything's unplugged, which he says with the first real enthusiasm I've ever heard from him. I understand maybe a third of it. I put my chin in my hand and nod in the right places, and halfway through I notice I'm not pretending anymore. He's just so into it.

<<silently>>
<<set _msgs = [
{ who: $brian, text: "...and that's why you discharge it first. Always. Uh. Sorry. That was a lot." },
{ who: $sister, text: "No! No, it was great. I didn't know electricity could be scary. I mean, I did. But like, sneaky scary." },
{ who: $brian, text: "It's very sneaky. Do you, uh. Do you want to see something?" },
]>>
<</silently>>
<<Chat _msgs>>

He opens his bottom drawer, and it's soldering irons. Seven of them, in a row, on a folded towel. Each one has a little label in his handwriting. One of them is labeled <i>do not lend</i>, and he tells me the whole story of why, and it involves a cousin and a model train and a fire department, and I laugh so hard I snort, and he almost looks at me.

I think, where $brian.name is from, that drawer was a proposal.

When I get up, he says thank you for the coffee three times, once while I'm still standing there, once when I'm a few steps away and once to my empty chair.

On the way back to my desk I look over at $player.name, just to see if he saw any of it. He's on the phone. Maybe he saw. I can't tell, and I hate that I want to know.

<<EndPov>>
